from pathlib import Path
import re
root=Path('/workspace/thesis/Chapters')
changed={}
def p(file, start, new):
 path=root/file
 if path not in changed:
  raw=path.read_bytes(); changed[path]=(raw,raw.decode().replace('\r\n','\n'))
 raw,t=changed[path]
 pattern=re.compile(r'(?m)^'+re.escape(start)+r'[^\n]*')
 hits=list(pattern.finditer(t))
 if len(hits)!=1: raise ValueError((file,start,len(hits)))
 t=t[:hits[0].start()]+new+t[hits[0].end():]
 changed[path]=(raw,t)

p('02_Background/chapter.tex','The inherited circuit',r'''The charge-pump and isolated resonant paths share one switching stage. Their interaction determines input-current shaping, power transfer, and commutation. The reference converters establish this operating principle; the USB-C adaptation adds startup, no-load, and load-transient requirements.''')
p('02_Background/01_Offline_ACDC_PFC.tex','The input stage',r'''A capacitor-input bridge rectifier conducts mainly near the mains-voltage peaks, producing narrow current pulses, harmonic distortion, and increased RMS current for a given real power. Input-current shaping extends conduction over the line cycle while supplying the DC link.''')
p('02_Background/01_Offline_ACDC_PFC.tex','In a conventional',r'''Conventional two-stage supplies separate input-current shaping and DC-link regulation from isolated output regulation. The charge-pump architecture shares switching devices between these functions, reducing active-stage component count but coupling output control to input-current quality.''')
p('02_Background/01_Offline_ACDC_PFC.tex','The revised plan',r'''The revised plan compares charge-pump and boost-PFC architectures in efficiency, component stress, power density, and control complexity \cite{projectOctoberPlan}. A numerical comparison requires matched operating points and power boundaries, measured device stress, and complete assembly volumes at verified continuous output. Control complexity includes sensing, actuation, startup, regulation, and fault handling. Only the charge-pump supply was tested; a matched boost-PFC benchmark remains outstanding.''')
p('02_Background/01_Offline_ACDC_PFC.tex','The supply must store',r'''The DC link buffers the mismatch between pulsating input power and load demand. Higher switching frequency reduces per-cycle energy but does not remove the twice-line-frequency storage and ripple requirements.''')
p('02_Background/01_Offline_ACDC_PFC.tex','In this converter',r'''The charge-pump branch shapes input current without a separate boost-PFC current loop. The DC link and secondary capacitance buffer the power imbalance. Because both paths share the switching sequence, input power quality must be evaluated with secondary-voltage control active.''')
p('02_Background/02_Charge_Pump_Principle.tex','This relationship depends',r'''Complete capacitor charge and discharge are required for this relationship. Low DC-link voltage, incomplete clamping, and interrupted switching reduce or alter the per-cycle charge. The 2021 reference adds an inductor and clamp paths to control these intervals while separating the pump and isolated-output tanks \cite{ammar2021led}.''')
p('02_Background/02_Charge_Pump_Principle.tex','The capacitor and inductor',r'''Capacitance sets the available charge per active cycle; inductance sets current slope, peak current, and clamp timing. Increasing capacitance can increase transfer, with corresponding pump-current and commutation demands on the shared half bridge.''')
p('02_Background/02_Charge_Pump_Principle.tex','Burst control changes',r'''Burst control varies active-cycle density at fixed carrier frequency. Charge transfer follows the pump sequence during each packet and ceases between packets. Constant density scales the average input conductance; density varying with line phase or secondary ripple distorts the input-current envelope.''')
p('02_Background/02_Charge_Pump_Principle.tex','Inherent PFC refers',r'''Inherent PFC describes the charge-transfer mechanism. Assembled-supply PF and THD remain dependent on load and burst control and require measurement.''')
p('02_Background/03_ClassDE_Resonant.tex','The second power path',r'''The isolated path comprises the half bridge, series resonant network, transformer, and secondary rectifier. The rectangular switch-node voltage excites approximately sinusoidal tank current. The rectifier charges the secondary bulk capacitor, whose voltage is regulated by the controller.''')
p('02_Background/03_ClassDE_Resonant.tex','The first-harmonic approximation',r'''The first-harmonic approximation represents the bridge by its fundamental voltage and the rectifier/load by an equivalent resistance. It supports initial gain and component sizing for approximately sinusoidal tank current. Burst transitions, discontinuous rectifier conduction, and no-load operation violate its steady-state load assumptions.''')
p('02_Background/03_ClassDE_Resonant.tex','The discrete inductor',r'''Discrete inductance and transformer leakage form the series branch; magnetizing inductance remains a separate branch. Although omitted from the first series-resonance estimate, magnetizing current contributes to primary RMS current, loss, and commutation, particularly at light load.''')
p('02_Background/03_ClassDE_Resonant.tex','Above resonance',r'''Above resonance, the loaded series tank presents an inductive impedance, providing lagging current for switch-node commutation during dead time. Pump current also contributes at the shared node and must be included in the soft-switching assessment.''')
p('02_Background/03_ClassDE_Resonant.tex','The reference LED-driver',r'''The reference LED-driver approximation neglects magnetizing current, whereas an LLC gain model retains finite magnetizing inductance \cite{ammar2021led}. The series approximation is used for initial sizing; the magnetizing branch and burst dynamics are retained when interpreting prototype switching and transients.''')
p('02_Background/04_Soft_Switching_GaN.tex','At \\SI',r'''At \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}, switching loss is sensitive to the energy dissipated per transition:''')
p('02_Background/04_Soft_Switching_GaN.tex','GaN allows',r'''Fast GaN transitions increase sensitivity to interconnect inductance, producing \(L\,\mathrm{d}i/\mathrm{d}t\) overshoot, and to capacitive coupling from the switch node into sensing and control. Gate-drive layout, decoupling, commutation-loop area, and return paths therefore affect switching behavior.''')
p('02_Background/04_Soft_Switching_GaN.tex','Dead time has',r'''Dead time must complete node commutation without excessive reverse conduction. An insufficient interval can leave residual turn-on voltage or allow cross conduction; an excessive interval increases loss. The required interval depends on bus voltage, current, capacitance, and tank state, so burst restart is assessed separately from established switching.''')
p('02_Background/04_Soft_Switching_GaN.tex','An integrated LMG2652',r'''The prototype uses an integrated LMG2652 half bridge. The inherited discrete-GaN and external-driver models differ in capacitance, delay, sensing response, and thermal paths. They establish operating principles; measurements establish switching behavior in the assembled stage.''')
p('02_Background/04_Soft_Switching_GaN.tex','The measurement must',r'''A ZVS assessment requires switch-node voltage aligned with the incoming gate command or device turn-on time, with adequate bandwidth and controlled probe loading, loop area, and common-mode error. Chapter~\ref{ch:results} applies these criteria to the available captures.''')
p('02_Background/05_Reference_2020.tex','For the present design',r'''Frequency changes affect both charge transfer and resonant gain. The prototype adds different magnetics, isolation, a downstream regulator, and sampled burst control; its complete-system performance is therefore measured independently.''')
p('02_Background/05_Reference_2020.tex','The charge-transfer analysis',r'''The charge-transfer analysis is retained. The following isolated-driver architecture provides the closer precedent, with separate pump and output paths sharing one half bridge.''')
p('02_Background/06_Reference_2021.tex','The 2021 LED driver',r'''The 2021 LED driver separates the charge-pump and isolated class-DE tanks while retaining a shared half bridge and driver \cite{ammar2021led}. Independent pump inductance and clamp paths permit more independent selection of isolated-stage gain, while the bus and switching sequence remain shared.''')
p('02_Background/06_Reference_2021.tex','The separate paths',r'''Pump current contributes directly to half-bridge commutation. In the reference, its amplitude can exceed the primary-referred output-tank current, and its inductive phase supports ZVS over much of the line cycle. Omitting this branch would understate available commutation current.''')
p('02_Background/06_Reference_2021.tex','The same burst-control',r'''Burst control was adapted from LED-current regulation to secondary-bulk-voltage regulation. A detachable USB-C load requires startup and no-load states that retain a usable bulk voltage through attachment and removal. The downstream stage supplies the selected VBUS setting.''')
p('02_Background/07_USBC_Context.tex','The USB-C output',r'''The USB-C stage negotiates and regulates the connector voltage, while its input demand is supplied through secondary storage by the resonant converter. Connector load changes therefore appear as bulk-bus disturbances at the upstream control loop.''')
p('02_Background/07_USBC_Context.tex','The isolated resonant stage',r'''The resonant stage supplies the secondary bulk bus upstream of the MPQ4242 output regulator. Fixed VBUS settings of \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} were tested. Bulk-voltage feedback enters the MCU at PA1; the connector-voltage measurements are downstream of this control loop.''')
p('02_Background/07_USBC_Context.tex','On attachment',r'''Load attachment depletes secondary storage before burst transfer increases. Load removal leaves residual tank energy to charge the bulk capacitor. At no load, repeated packets raise bulk voltage whenever their energy exceeds auxiliary and leakage consumption.''')
p('02_Background/07_USBC_Context.tex','Low-power startup',r'''Low-power startup, bulk-voltage thresholds, load-dependent burst limits, and overvoltage inhibition were implemented for these conditions and assessed in fixed-setting and transient tests. Formal USB-PD protocol, timing, and interoperability testing remains outstanding.''')
p('02_Background/08_Research_Gap.tex','The selected semiconductors',r'''The selected semiconductors, supplied magnetics, auxiliary rails, sensing, and output interface were integrated with a controller that starts before isolated feedback is available and limits no-load transfer. Connector voltage, upstream bulk voltage, input current, and temperature were measured separately to assess the combined hardware and control response.''')
p('02_Background/08_Research_Gap.tex','The assembled prototype',r'''The assembled prototype was tested with the developed voltage-control firmware. Later circuit corrections are documented in schematic V1.1. The resulting operating requirements are defined in the next chapter.''')

p('03_Requirements_Architecture/chapter.tex','The load behavior',r'''The USB-C load requires controlled startup, loaded regulation, load removal, and no-load energy limitation. These states define the hardware interfaces and verification requirements. Power targets are distinguished from implemented controller settings and measured capability.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/01_Use_Case.tex','Operation begins',r'''The operating sequence covers unloaded startup, load attachment, loaded operation, removal, and commanded shutdown. Residual resonant and capacitive energy after removal makes no-load control and overvoltage response explicit design requirements.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/02_Electrical_Specifications.tex','Table~',r'''Table~\ref{tab:req-design-specifications} distinguishes power criteria from the original MCU timing and feedback parameters. The fixed carrier is gated into bursts; burst scheduling and sensor/control updates operate on separate time scales. Sections~\ref{sec:hw-v2} and \ref{sec:ctrl-pwr2-port} describe the V2 implementation, with power-stage enable assigned to PA11.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/02_Electrical_Specifications.tex','The output-voltage settings',r'''The USB-C settings apply downstream of the regulated bulk bus. Bulk-voltage feedback (PA1) controls the upstream supply; the primary half-bridge-current input (PA2) supports load classification. Neither input directly measures the corresponding connector quantity.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/03_Functional_Requirements.tex','The functional requirements',r'''Requirement identifiers connect the implemented control functions and measured performance to the results assessment.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/03_Functional_Requirements.tex','The pin notation',r'''Controller interfaces are introduced in \cref{sec:intro-controller-basics}. On the original prototype, PA10 gates switching bursts, PA1 senses secondary bulk voltage, PA0 senses primary voltage, and PA2 senses half-bridge current. Direct memory access (DMA) buffers the ADC scan; the universal asynchronous receiver-transmitter (UART) carries commands and status.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/03_Functional_Requirements.tex','Chapter~',r'''Chapter~\ref{ch:results} compares implementation with measured electrical behavior. Section~\ref{sec:revised-plan-criteria} assigns AP identifiers to the additional V2/V3 development criteria, separate from the baseline functions.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/04_USBC_Interface.tex','The V1.1 schematic',r'''The V1.1 MPQ4242GVE-0000-AEC1-P output stage is supplied from \texttt{V\_PW\_OUTPUT\_BULK} and feeds \texttt{USB\_PD\_OUT} and connector VBUS at J1. Configuration channels CC1 and CC2 support attachment detection and power negotiation.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/04_USBC_Interface.tex','The output section',r'''The stage includes local capacitance, current sensing, and VBUS and signal protection. The I2C service header exposes clock \texttt{PD\_SCL} and data \texttt{PD\_SDA}. The MCU does not configure the MPQ4242 over I2C; the fitted controller configuration and sink or trigger determine the tested connector setting.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/04_USBC_Interface.tex','In this thesis',r'''Secondary bulk voltage denotes the resonant-stage output regulated through PA1; VBUS denotes the measured connector output. The downstream converter changes mean voltage and ripple, so these nodes are reported separately.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/05_Safety_Protection.tex','Controls were added',r'''Precharge applies limited excitation until the isolated feedback supply is established. High-impedance classification lowers the burst limit and voltage window. Eligible light/no-load overvoltage invokes soft holdoff within the hard margin; other overvoltage conditions latch overvoltage protection (OVP) until the defined clear or reset action. Burst enable (PA10) is held low to inhibit further excitation, without discharging stored energy. Chapter~\ref{ch:control} and Appendix~\ref{app:equations} specify the conditions.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/05_Safety_Protection.tex','The primary half-bridge-current',r'''Primary half-bridge-current feedback (PA2) supports load classification and diagnostics. The configurable current trip is disabled by default, and the active voltage loop does not call legacy foldback helpers. Any current-limited test must identify the active limiter: output controller, AC source, electronic load, or explicitly enabled firmware threshold.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/06_System_Architecture.tex','The MCU on',r'''On the original prototype, PA8 and PA9 generate complementary carrier commands and PA10 gates average transfer through burst enable. Primary-voltage (PA0), secondary-voltage (PA1), and half-bridge-current (PA2) signals are acquired by a continuous ADC scan and buffered by DMA. Sampling is asynchronous to the carrier.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/06_System_Architecture.tex','The auxiliary rails',r'''Auxiliary rails supply the gate drive, MCU, sensing, and output interface. Bring-up therefore establishes these rails and the inhibited carrier before feedback calibration and low-power transfer. Closed-loop operation follows once isolated feedback is valid.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/06_System_Architecture.tex','The two resonant paths',r'''The resonant paths meet at the switch node. Burst control consequently changes both secondary transfer and the mains-current envelope; the next chapter analyzes this coupling.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/07_Operating_Modes.tex','One burst limit',r'''Startup, loaded regulation, and no-load energy limitation require distinct burst limits. The original prototype uses PA10 as the active-high burst-enable GPIO. UART provides command/status access, and ADC readings are buffered by DMA.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/08_Verification_Matrix.tex','Section~',r'''Section~\ref{sec:res-compliance} compares requirements with completed measurements; Section~\ref{sec:revised-plan-criteria} extends the scope to V2/V3 development. Firmware implementation establishes the commanded function, while measurements establish its electrical response.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/09_Revised_Plan_Criteria.tex','The revised July',r'''The revised July--October plan extends bring-up into V2 evaluation, converter adjustment, and V3 edge-card development \cite{projectOctoberPlan}. The criteria below cover baseline repetition, diagnosis of the output-power limit, controlled design changes, and readiness for final evaluation.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/09_Revised_Plan_Criteria.tex','Table~',r'''Table~\ref{tab:req-revised-plan-criteria} defines the additional design and test criteria, including thermal, stability, and volume measurements required beyond output power.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/09_Revised_Plan_Criteria.tex','A density comparison',r'''Power density requires verified output and complete assembly dimensions. Section~\ref{sec:method-density} uses a common rectangular envelope including magnetics, connectors, supports, component height, required spacing, and cooling hardware. V2 and V3 assembly dimensions remain necessary; they cannot be inferred from the V1.0 outline.''')
p('03_Requirements_Architecture/09_Revised_Plan_Criteria.tex','The plan includes',r'''Planar-magnetic optimization is a learning objective. The supplied designs were retained, so this thesis evaluates their integration and effect on converter operation. Original electromagnetic design is attributed in Section~\ref{sec:intro-contributions}.''')

p('04_Topology_Analysis/chapter.tex','The controller acts',r'''The shared switching stage couples pump charge transfer, DC-link energy balance, and isolated resonant transfer. Reference equations and fitted values are used to estimate power transfer, commutation, and stress, and to define the corresponding bench checks.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/01_Topology_Overview.tex','The rectified input',r'''The rectified input follows the mains envelope, while the DC link stores energy and supplies the half bridge. Pump capacitance \(\CP\), inductance \(\LP\), and steering/clamp paths transfer energy between these nodes during each enabled cycle.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/01_Topology_Overview.tex','From \\texttt',r'''The isolated branch is excited from \texttt{VSW} through series capacitance \(\CRES\), discrete inductance, and transformer leakage. The transformer includes a separate magnetizing branch; secondary rectification supplies the bulk capacitor and MPQ4242 stage. Throughout the analysis, \(V_{\mathrm{sec}}\) denotes bulk voltage and VBUS denotes connector voltage.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/01_Topology_Overview.tex','The two branch currents',r'''Signed branch currents sum at the half bridge and jointly determine device current and commutation. The DC link couples collected pump power to secondary demand over longer intervals.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/01_Topology_Overview.tex','The carrier remains',r'''The active carrier remains at \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}; burst duration is controlled by PA10 on the original prototype. Secondary-bulk feedback \(V_{\mathrm{sec}}\) enters PA1, optional primary-voltage feed-forward enters PA0, and primary-current feedback at PA2 distinguishes loaded and no-load states. Burst control changes both average secondary transfer and pump charge-transfer density.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/02_Switching_Cycle.tex','Each enabled cycle',r'''Each enabled cycle commutates the switch node between the DC-link rails. The pump sequence comprises charge-transfer, clamp, and freewheeling intervals, while the isolated tank carries inductive current within an established burst. Table~\ref{tab:analysis-pump-intervals} summarizes the six reference intervals \cite{ammar2021led}.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/02_Switching_Cycle.tex','In that ideal cycle',r'''The ideal pump-capacitor excursion is approximately zero to the rectified line voltage. Transferred charge sets the cycle-averaged input current; \(\LP\) sets the current slope and completion time.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/02_Switching_Cycle.tex','During dead time',r'''During dead time, signed branch currents charge and discharge device and node capacitances. Pump current can support commutation at low isolated load, whereas opposing branch currents reduce the available net current.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/02_Switching_Cycle.tex','The repeating sequence',r'''Burst restart begins from the inductor currents and capacitor voltages left by the idle interval. It therefore requires separate assessment from the periodic sequence, particularly during sparse no-load packets.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/03_Line_Cycle_PFC.tex','Constant \\(b\\)',r'''Constant \(b\) scales input conductance without changing the ideal envelope. Line-phase variation of \(b(t)\), caused by bulk-voltage ripple response or feed-forward, modifies current shape. Power quality must therefore be assessed with the final controller.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/03_Line_Cycle_PFC.tex','The DC link must',r'''The DC link must remain sufficiently above the rectified-line peak to avoid unintended direct conduction. Ripple, diode drops, finite charging current, and clamp timing alter the actual excursion. The \(f_s\CP\) conductance is an ideal estimate; measured PF/THD includes pump and controller effects.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/04_Pump_Capacitor.tex','The prototype has',r'''The nominal \SI{2}{\nano\farad} pump capacitor sets charge per complete active cycle and maximum ideal conductance at a given line voltage. Burst control can reduce transfer by omitting cycles but cannot increase charge within an unchanged cycle.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/04_Pump_Capacitor.tex','The part also',r'''Selection also depends on pulse-current capability, voltage margin, effective capacitance, dielectric loss, ESR, and interconnect impedance. Ringing, incomplete clamping, and transients add to the nominal rectified-line excursion.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/04_Pump_Capacitor.tex','Increasing \\(\\CP',r'''Increasing \(\CP\) raises transfer and pump current at fixed frequency and changes the inductance required to complete charge transfer. Capacitance and inductance must therefore be evaluated together against clamp loss and semiconductor stress.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/05_Pump_Inductor.tex','The result is',r'''This estimate assumes the triangular, clamped current sequence of the reference analysis \cite{ammar2021led}.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/05_Pump_Inductor.tex','For \\(\\CP',r'''For \(\CP=\SI{2}{\nano\farad}\) and \(f_s=\SI{1}{\mega\hertz}\), the estimate is \SI{31.25}{\micro\henry}. Supplied measurements of \SI{34.26}{\micro\henry} and \SI{33.09}{\micro\henry} are close to this value (Section~\ref{sec:mag-inherited}); in-circuit current and clamp timing determine agreement with the assumed sequence.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/05_Pump_Inductor.tex','The estimate places',r'''The carrier lies above ideal pump resonance, giving inductive current that can support shared-half-bridge commutation over much of the line cycle.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/05_Pump_Inductor.tex','Lower inductance',r'''Lower inductance increases current slope, peak stress, and possible clamp conduction. Higher inductance can prevent completion of the capacitor excursion. In-circuit waveforms are required to establish the timing margin.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/06_DC_Link.tex','The expression gives',r'''The expression gives ripple amplitude; approximate peak-to-peak ripple is twice this value.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/06_DC_Link.tex','More link capacitance',r'''Increasing link capacitance reduces line-frequency ripple and improves pump-voltage margin, but increases stored energy during startup, load removal, and abnormal switching.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/06_DC_Link.tex','The voltage rating',r'''Measured bus mean, ripple, and maximum establish capacitor-voltage margin and half-bridge excitation. Control can redistribute the single-phase energy ripple but cannot eliminate its storage requirement.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/07_Resonant_Stage.tex','The nominal resonance',r'''The nominal resonance lies close to the \SI{1}{\mega\hertz} carrier. Loaded gain and commutation also depend on the magnetizing branch and pump current.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/07_Resonant_Stage.tex','Here \\(P_',r'''Here \(P_{\mathrm{sec}}\) includes downstream demand and losses. Using connector power alone would understate tank loading.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/07_Resonant_Stage.tex','These expressions',r'''These first-harmonic relationships provide an initial component and gain check at the assumed load \cite{ammar2021led}.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/07_Resonant_Stage.tex','The supplied magnetizing',r'''The supplied magnetizing inductance is approximately \SIrange{140}{151}{\micro\henry}, about five to six times nominal series inductance. Its effect on input impedance increases at light load and burst transitions. Because Equation~\eqref{eq:analysis-series-gain} omits this branch, measured gain discrepancies require a fuller model before revising series components.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/07_Resonant_Stage.tex','In the installed circuit',r'''Installed behavior also depends on transformer variant and polarity, rectifier conduction, and frequency-dependent parasitics. Chapters~\ref{ch:magnetics} and \ref{ch:hardware} describe the integration.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/08_ZVS.tex','The charge condition',r'''The charge condition must be met with the required polarity and before turn-on; charge magnitude alone does not establish ZVS.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/08_ZVS.tex','Both power paths',r'''Both paths contribute to \(i_{\mathrm{dt}}\). In the modified reference, inductive pump current can exceed primary-referred output current and provide much of the commutation charge \cite{ammar2021led}. Its prototype contribution varies with line voltage and burst history; isolated load and magnetizing currents depend on transformer impedance and rectifier state.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/08_ZVS.tex','A nominal resonance',r'''Near-carrier resonance does not establish ZVS across the operating range. Tolerances shift resonance, pump current falls near line zero crossings, and idle intervals can remove circulating current. These conditions require separate switching captures even at fixed dead time.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/08_ZVS.tex','Dead time should',r'''Dead time must complete commutation without excessive reverse conduction. The effective interval includes command timing, driver propagation, and device response. A quantitative margin requires calibrated node capacitance and timing, which the inherited model does not provide. Section~\ref{sec:res-zvs} assesses the available captures.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/09_Device_Stress.tex','The primary resonant-path',r'''The primary branch already includes magnetizing current: \(i_{\mathrm{res,p}}=i_{\mathrm{load,p}}+i_m\). Adding \(i_m\) to the complete branch again would double-count it.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/09_Device_Stress.tex','The pump can',r'''Pump current can impose substantial half-bridge stress at modest connector current. Calibrated primary-current feedback at PA2 supports load classification; it does not directly measure connector current.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/09_Device_Stress.tex','The same current pulses',r'''Pump-capacitor, inductor, and steering-diode stress depends on line phase, link voltage, and charge-transfer completion. Isolated-branch circulating current contributes winding and resonant-capacitor heating, while secondary rectifier stress depends on conduction intervals and storage voltage.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/09_Device_Stress.tex','A command change',r'''Driving the original prototype's burst-enable output PA10 low inhibits excitation but does not remove stored tank energy. The hard OVP margin must therefore include sensing and actuation delays and residual transfer after inhibition.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/10_USBC_Adaptation.tex','The resonant converter',r'''The resonant converter supplies the downstream regulator. Secondary demand varies with connector current and VBUS setting; the MCU regulates bulk voltage while the output stage regulates VBUS.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/10_USBC_Adaptation.tex','Burst enable changes',r'''Burst enable varies active-interval duration and spacing at fixed carrier and tank values. Each omitted cycle also removes pump charge transfer, coupling secondary regulation to the input-current envelope.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/10_USBC_Adaptation.tex','Under load',r'''Bulk-voltage depletion calls for increased transfer under load. At no load, a lower voltage window and burst cap limit accumulation. Repeated low voltage temporarily relaxes the cap for load attachment; OVP independently selects soft holdoff or hard inhibition.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/10_USBC_Adaptation.tex','The output capacitor',r'''Secondary capacitance supports load transients and absorbs residual tank energy. Increasing it reduces packet-induced voltage steps but increases startup energy and slows voltage-based load detection. Chapter~\ref{ch:control} applies this tradeoff to storage, command limits, and state logic.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/11_Design_Summary.tex','The pump values',r'''The pump values place ideal charge transfer in the intended power range, while series values place nominal resonance near the carrier. Magnetizing current, burst transitions, and DC-link ripple determine departures from this estimate.''')
p('04_Topology_Analysis/11_Design_Summary.tex','These checks are',r'''Pump capacitance and line voltage set charge transfer; link capacitance buffers mains-energy ripple; the tank and ratio set secondary excitation; both branch currents determine commutation and heating. Chapter~\ref{ch:simulation} applies these relationships to the inherited models, and Chapter~\ref{ch:results} evaluates the assembled converter.''')

p('05_Modeling_Simulation/chapter.tex','The main circuit',r'''The inherited models and analytical relationships are used to estimate charge transfer, resonant operation, and parameter sensitivity. They do not represent the complete prototype hardware and controller. Complete-system performance is therefore established by laboratory measurements rather than a calibrated final-board model.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/01_Objectives.tex','Different models',r'''Charge-transfer analysis estimates input-power scale, and the resonant equivalent circuit estimates active-cycle gain and phase. Clamp timing and burst transitions require a switched model; efficiency and temperature additionally require calibrated loss and thermal models. Each inherited model is used within this scope.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/01_Objectives.tex','The supplied LTspice',r'''The inherited LTspice circuits describe the predecessor rectifier. Supplied magnetic measurements provide the inductance and transformer parameters used in Chapter~\ref{ch:analysis}. Their application to the prototype accounts for the changed topology and installed components.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/01_Objectives.tex','For the final',r'''For the USB-C circuit and voltage-control firmware, analytical expectations are compared with measured bus ripple, turn-on current, packet energy, and response to component or command changes.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/02_Uploaded_LTspice_Files.tex','The main inherited',r'''The inherited \sourcefile{Simplified_for_journal_revision.asc} and \sourcefile{Simplified_for_journal_revision_2.asc} circuits represent the high-voltage PFC rectifier: source, bridge, pump capacitor, half bridge, series tank, rectifier, and resistive load. The prototype adds isolation, a separate pump inductor, the USB-C stage, and MCU burst control, changing both topology and parameters.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/02_Uploaded_LTspice_Files.tex','Updating the inherited',r'''Replacing the \SI{158}{\micro\henry} element alone would omit the transformer, reflected load, and revised interconnections. The predecessor regulates a high-voltage output; the prototype has separate isolated bulk and connector VBUS nodes. Model comparison requires corresponding nodes and power boundaries.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/02_Uploaded_LTspice_Files.tex','The \\sourcefile{Power',r'''The \sourcefile{Power_loss_breakdown.asc} variants and device libraries describe the predecessor's loss calculations. Prototype efficiency is established from measured input and output power. Appendix~\ref{app:modeling} identifies the inherited files and their roles.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/03_GaN_Model.tex','To predict switching',r'''A fitted-stage switching model requires voltage-dependent output charge, gate/enable timing, reverse conduction, and interconnect impedance. Temperature prediction additionally requires package, PCB, and cooling paths. At MHz frequency, transition-energy error directly affects calculated loss, so device-name substitution is insufficient.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/04_Simulation_Setup.tex','Input current',r'''Fourier and measurement directives calculate input current, RMS voltage/current, power, efficiency, and PF. The retained logs and RAW files are inherited results; a matching final-board model was not rerun.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/04_Simulation_Setup.tex','For a final',r'''A final switched-model comparison requires settling, an evaluation interval spanning line and burst behavior, and a time step resolving commutation. Time-step convergence and energy balance must be established before comparison with measured waveforms.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/05_Simulation_Cases.tex','A prototype model',r'''The prototype model requires the pump inductor and clamp sequence; separate transformer leakage and magnetizing branches; turns ratio, rectifier conduction, and storage; and the controller's sampling, burst timing, and isolated-feedback startup delay.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/05_Simulation_Cases.tex','A fixed resistor',r'''A fixed resistor at connector voltage does not reproduce the downstream regulator's bulk-bus demand. Attachment and removal also change connected capacitance. The assumed power, impedance, and load dynamics must therefore be specified.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/05_Simulation_Cases.tex','Established bursts',r'''Established-burst operation at a known load provides the initial calibration point, followed by line-cycle buffering, load transients, and high-impedance operation. This final-hardware simulation remains unfinished; the following calculations provide analytical expectations for measurement.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/06_Steady_State.tex','Steady operation',r'''Three time scales govern operation: carrier-scale phase, clamp timing, and packet energy; burst-scale average transfer; and line-cycle storage imbalance in the DC link and secondary capacitors. Resolving one scale does not establish behavior at the others.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/06_Steady_State.tex','The nominal series',r'''Nominal series resonance is near \SI{0.989}{\mega\hertz}. Turn-on phase also depends on magnetizing current, pump current, and rectifier state, which must be included in commutation prediction.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/06_Steady_State.tex','The model would',r'''Calibration requires matched input and secondary demand, followed by separate comparisons of voltage envelopes and switching waveforms. Mean VBUS agreement alone does not validate bulk ripple or commutation current. Chapter~\ref{ch:results} reports these quantities separately.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/07_Startup_NoLoad.tex','The isolated feedback',r'''Limited precharge establishes power for isolated feedback before voltage control. A startup model must include this supply delay; assuming initially valid feedback omits the condition addressed by precharge.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/07_Startup_NoLoad.tex','This relationship',r'''Secondary capacitance reduces the voltage rise per packet. Quantitative overshoot prediction requires deposited energy, effective capacitance, and controller delay, including excitation delivered before inhibition.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/07_Startup_NoLoad.tex','When the MCU',r'''Driving the original prototype's burst-enable output PA10 low inhibits further excitation while stored energy continues to circulate. The no-load guard reduces repeated packets; soft holdoff and latched OVP block further commands. Their margins require a switched model with the measured circuit and timing.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/08_Parameter_Sweeps.tex','The first-order',r'''The first-order equations estimate analytical sensitivity; no final-board tolerance sweep was completed. For continuous ideal charge transfer,''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/08_Parameter_Sweeps.tex','for continuous ideal',r'''The quadratic line-voltage dependence changes power transfer without a component change. Burst operation adds active-cycle-density dependence, which must be included in matched comparisons.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/08_Parameter_Sweeps.tex','For example',r'''A 5\% increase in either series parameter gives an approximately 2.5\% first-order resonance reduction. These calculated sensitivities are illustrative, rather than measured or simulated results. Near-carrier operation makes the corresponding phase shift relevant to commutation.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/08_Parameter_Sweeps.tex','The effect on',r'''Increasing link capacitance reduces ripple and increases stored energy. Increasing secondary capacitance reduces packet-induced voltage rise but increases startup energy. Storage changes therefore affect both steady operation and transients.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/08_Parameter_Sweeps.tex','These trends',r'''Saturation, nonlinear capacitance, rectifier conduction, and control-state changes limit these sensitivities. Combined effects require a switched model or matched hardware comparison.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/08_Parameter_Sweeps.tex','The revised plan',r'''The revised plan \cite{projectOctoberPlan} specifies baseline repetition followed by one-parameter or one-part changes under matched input, output setting, load, firmware, and thermal conditions. Candidate variables include sensing calibration, timing, control limits, pump and tank values, gate drive, and cooling. The included controller has a fixed carrier; frequency and dead-time experiments require a separately identified configuration. The planned progression from \SI{85}{\watt} through \SI{90}{\watt}, \SI{95}{\watt}, and \SI{100}{\watt} remains conditional on electrical-stress and temperature limits.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/09_Loss_Estimation.tex','A complete loss',r'''The supply loss balance includes semiconductor conduction and switching, pump and rectifier diodes, magnetic copper and core loss, capacitor ESR, auxiliary circuits, and the downstream regulator. Burst-dependent active loss and persistent auxiliary/idle consumption require stated power boundaries and burst state.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/09_Loss_Estimation.tex','Prototype efficiency',r'''Paired input/output measurements at the Chapter~\ref{ch:results} boundaries establish efficiency and aggregate loss. They do not separate GaN, magnetic, and output-stage dissipation. Thermal images locate investigation areas; surface temperature alone does not quantify component loss.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/09_Loss_Estimation.tex','Finding the output-power',r'''Diagnosis must distinguish dissipation from the mechanism preventing further load increase. Candidate limits include transformer copper/core loss, pump loss, GaN switching and conduction, rectification, interconnects, auxiliary consumption, heat removal, device stress, and command/protection limits. Temperature and controller-state observations identify follow-up measurements but do not individually establish the limiting mechanism.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/09_Loss_Estimation.tex','The present measurements',r'''The available measurements establish aggregate loss. A V2 component-loss budget and explanation of the reported \SI{15}{\watt} shortfall require a repeatable operating point with matched waveforms and temperatures, followed by controlled single changes. This procedure must also identify the highest repeatable output and remaining limit if the continuous target is not reached.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/10_Data_Limitations.tex','The analysis provides',r'''The analysis estimates pump charge-transfer scale, nominal resonance, and parameter sensitivity. DC-link ripple, magnetizing current, burst timing, and residual tank energy provide a framework for interpreting bench behavior.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/10_Data_Limitations.tex','To predict the prototype',r'''A predictive prototype model requires the installed branches, load dynamics, parasitics, and timing, calibrated against startup, load-step, no-load, and switching captures. Efficiency and temperature prediction additionally require loss and thermal calibration.''')
p('05_Modeling_Simulation/10_Data_Limitations.tex','The next chapters',r'''The following magnetics, hardware, and control chapters define the installed circuit and operating conditions required for that calibration. Laboratory tests establish progress toward the power and thermal targets.''')

p('06_Magnetics_Integration/chapter.tex','The converter was',r'''The supplied planar magnetics fixed the initial inductance, turns ratio, terminal arrangement, and assembly envelope. Integration into the power PCB adds interconnect impedance, mounting constraints, and thermal coupling that affect installed behavior.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/chapter.tex','The supplied magnetics',r'''The following hardware chapter describes the power-board integration and bring-up changes. Installed magnetic-path measurements remain necessary to separate magnetic, interconnect, and switching losses.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/01_Provenance.tex','The earlier work',r'''The inherited work covered core selection, winding geometry, air-gap studies, and loss calculations using MATLAB and two-dimensional FEM in COMSOL Multiphysics 6.2 \cite{comsol62}. The supplied cores and windings were retained; this thesis covers electrical connection, mechanical integration, and converter analysis using the supplied values. The table distinguishes these contributions.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/02_Inherited_Specifications.tex','Both inductors',r'''Both inductors are close to the \SI{33}{\micro\henry} target. Transformer leakage is about \SI{16.5}{\micro\henry}, magnetizing inductance spans approximately \SI{140}{\micro\henry} to \SI{151}{\micro\henry}, and the turns ratio is near 8:1. Leakage was included in selecting the additional series inductance.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/02_Inherited_Specifications.tex','The assembly information',r'''The installed A/B combination is unidentified, so calculations use the supplied range or a stated nominal value with the \cref{eq:app-turns-ratio} convention. AC-resistance loss estimates also require the original measurement frequency and fixture. Identifying and remeasuring the fitted parts would resolve these uncertainties.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/03_Integration_Requirements.tex','The inductor and transformer',r'''The inductor carries pump current; the transformer transfers isolated resonant power. Component and interconnect resistance and inductance are evaluated as complete outgoing-and-return loops.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/03_Integration_Requirements.tex','The transformer also',r'''Primary-to-secondary separation must be maintained through the winding PCB, solder joints, supports, and adjacent main-board copper. Isolation assessment therefore covers the complete assembly.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/03_Integration_Requirements.tex','The connections',r'''The schematic in \Cref{fig:schematic-v11-power-stage} and assembly in \cref{fig:final-prototype-main-photo} identify the converter integration. A component-specific terminal drawing is still required for reproducible assembly.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/04_Mounting_Concept.tex','The supplied cores',r'''The cores and winding PCBs were mounted as subassemblies. Terminal positions and dimensions constrained the main-board interface and surrounding assembly/rework access.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/04_Mounting_Concept.tex','The magnetic assembly',r'''Mechanical support must avoid loading solder joints or altering the core gap through mounting pressure and PCB bending. The photographed arrangement requires a defined fastening procedure and post-mounting air-gap check to establish repeatability.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/05_Footprint_Interconnection.tex','The footprint is',r'''Pads, vias, terminal links, and return paths form part of the resonant loop, adding resistance and inductance. Footprint selection therefore affects electrical behavior as well as mounting.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/05_Footprint_Interconnection.tex','Transformer polarity',r'''Transformer polarity must match the secondary rectifier connections. The schematic names the winding nodes, but the available information does not map every terminal of the installed A/B variant. Reproduction requires terminal and polarity verification in addition to the gain estimate from turns ratio.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/06_Mechanical_Assembly.tex','The resonant loop',r'''Terminal-link movement and longer winding connections alter resonant-loop impedance. Core alignment, spacing, PCB bending, and contact changes can also affect the installed magnetic path.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/06_Mechanical_Assembly.tex','The supplied values',r'''The supplied \cref{tab:mag-inherited-measurements} values were retained because post-installation inductance and detailed rework measurements were unavailable. Main-board and link parasitics make the calculated resonance an initial estimate; refinement requires measurement at the installed terminals.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/07_Isolation_Clearance.tex','The transformer separates',r'''Installed creepage and clearance can be limited by solder fillets, temporary wiring, supports, exposed core surfaces, or the main PCB. Winding-board spacing alone does not establish the complete isolation path.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/07_Isolation_Clearance.tex','The main-board',r'''Main-board spacing and the laboratory insulation check are described in \Cref{sec:hw-layout,sec:hw-overview}. Full insulation assessment additionally requires documented transformer spacing, material group, and standardized dielectric testing. The functional isolation path does not establish these margins.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/08_Parasitics.tex','The installed connections',r'''Common-mode current follows \(i_{\mathrm{CM}}=C_{\mathrm{ps}}\,dv/dt\). Interwinding capacitance, adjacent copper, and probe loading therefore affect secondary-noise captures. Numerical attribution requires installed-assembly impedance and capacitance measurements.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/09_Thermal_Integration.tex','The temperature',r'''Magnetic temperature depends on winding, core, and terminal losses and thermal coupling from GaN devices, rectifiers, auxiliary supplies, copper, and airflow. Inductance and turns ratio do not establish an installed thermal limit.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/09_Thermal_Integration.tex','The board',r'''The images in \cref{sec:method-thermal,sec:res-thermal} compare surface-temperature patterns at tested outputs. They do not separate core and winding losses or resolve internal winding maxima. A continuous rating requires a timed thermal record on the identified assembly.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/10_Verification.tex','The supplied measurements',r'''The supplied measurements characterize the components before installation. Converter tests demonstrate assembled-path power transfer, while installed inductance and individual thermal and isolation margins remain unmeasured.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/11_Limitations.tex','The approximately',r'''The approximately \SI{33}{\micro\henry} pump inductance and \SI{16.5}{\micro\henry} transformer leakage provided the initial design values. Interconnect and mounting effects remain part of the installed impedance.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/11_Limitations.tex','The next step',r'''Further characterization requires fitted-part and terminal identification, in-circuit inductance measurement, complete-path isolation checks, and a stabilized thermal run. These measurements support reproducible assembly and loss-driven revision.''')
p('06_Magnetics_Integration/11_Limitations.tex','Secondary-winding',r'''Secondary-winding heating and limited power increase at higher load made transformer optimization a priority. The operating prototypes retained the inherited magnetics; replacement planar parts were not installed within the project time. The smaller V3 layout therefore establishes neither reduced magnetic loss nor increased continuous output.''')

